pkg/proc, pkg/terminal: results in registers, hit counting, JSON frames, longer strings

A tracepoint's return line read its results from their debug
information, which optimized code rarely has at a return, so most
results, error results above all, printed "(unreadable empty OP
stack)". At a RET instruction the results are in the registers Go's
internal ABI returns them in, whatever the debug information says:
EvalScope.RegABIResults assigns them as cmd/compile/abi-internal.md
does (integer and floating point registers in order, structs by field,
strings, slices and interfaces as their words, the stack for what does
not fit) and reads them, on amd64 and arm64. A result the debug
information leaves unreadable at a return breakpoint is read so; one
the ABI returns on the stack, or at a return that is not a RET (after
deferred calls), says why and that a build without optimizations reads
it. The results load following one pointer, so an error shows its
message:

	>> goroutine(1): [l] main.lookup => ("",error(*errors.errorString) *{s: "empty key"})

breakpoints said "(enabled, hits counted by the runtime)" while the
runtime of a detsched program counted a breakpoint's hits, but nothing
when it did not, nor after the breakpoint was written at the hit before
the one wanted. api.Breakpoint.RuntimeCounted now holds as long as the
runtime counts, and RuntimeNotCounted says why it does not count a
breakpoint with a hit condition (another condition, a per-goroutine
count, an operator it does not count, several addresses, no entry
tick, not at the entry, all slots taken):

	Breakpoint n (enabled, hits counted by Delve, one trap each: it has a condition besides the hit count) at ...

In the JSON output, breakpoints prints a record of its own, with
counted_by ("runtime" or "delve") and not_runtime_counted.

The runtime did not count the hits of any function of a build with
-gcflags=all=-N -l, which the debug: lines of detsched now run: its
entry tick branches past a jump to the call to runtime.detPreempt,
which detEntryTick did not follow. It now follows unconditional jumps
from either side of the branch.

The JSON output had two shapes for a stack: the stack record's
"frames", and a breakpoint record's "stack" for on NAME stack. The
breakpoint record now has "frames" too.

Strings were cut at 64 bytes, which cut keys and values. print, args -v,
locals -v and on NAME print show 1024 bytes, and tracepoint lines,
args and locals 256, unless config max-string-len says otherwise.

The results in registers and the JSON and string changes are generic
fixes for upstream Delve; the hit counting is detsched's (0004).

